## 1. Introduction
Among the developed various active materials, Fe/Ni/Co metal-organic frameworks (Fe/Co/Ni-MOFs) have aroused tremendous interests for OER due to their large surface area, tunable textures, highly porous structure, and abundant intrinsic active metal sites.[7] However, most of bulk
With the increasing energy crisis and environmental pollution, the clean and sustainable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ energy has been widely deemed as the appealing candidate for the next generation energy carrier. Currently, electrochemical water splitting is one of the promising strategies for scalable production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .[1] However, the inherent sluggish kinetics of the electrochemical water
MOFs usually display poor electrical conductivity and slow mass permeability, and only partial metal sites can be utilized in electrocatalytic reactions, which highly limits their electrocatalytic properties.[8] Therefore, to improve the conductivity of MOFs, much work has been done to hybridize MOFs with secondary highly conductive supports or convert MOF crystals into conductive metal oxides or heteroatom-doped porous carbons by thermal pyrolysis.[9] Unfortunately, these methods may block the micropores of MOFs, or make metal ions aggregation to nanoparticles, thus heavily sacrificing the intrinsic metal active sites for catalysis.[6a,10] Under such background, constructing large surface area 2D nanosheets or 1D nanorods is considered to be a promising strategy to enhance the electrocatalytic performance of MOFs, because such structures can increase the exposed unsaturated metal active sites, thus strengthen electron transfer and mass transport.[11] Up to now, facile synthesis of ultrathin MOF nanosheets or nanorods with good crystallinity and morphology is still a challenge.
On the other hand, LDHs based on Fe/Co/Ni metal ions have attracted increased attention in energy-related fields owing to their abundant reserves, low-cost, tunable composition, and tailorable orientation of active sites. LDHs have an edge-sharing octahedral $\mathrm{\DeltaMO_{6}}$ layer-stacking crystal structure, which has been proved to be the highly active sites for the OER.[12] However, the poor conductivity, low electron, and charge transfer ability and insufficient active edge sites of LDHs, especially for the bulk form of LDHs, significantly restrict their wildly electrocatalytic application. In this regard, transformation of bulk LDH structure into the corresponding large surface area nanostructure to expose more active sites, such as ultrathin 2D nanosheets, will significantly enhance the electrocatalytic performance.[13] In addition, hybridizing LDHs with MOF structures is also a promising way to enhance electrocatalytic activity.[14] Notably, LDHs have adjustable and reasonable layer spacing, which allows organic ligands to coordinate with interlayer metal ions, thus making them a promising precursor to form MOFs.[11c,15] It should be pointed out that these reported works mainly focused on the complete transformation of 2D LDH to MOF nanosheets, or partial transformation to core-shell stuctures. There are scarce reports on the fabrication of 2D/1D LDH-MOF hybrid nanostructures.
Herein, considering the structural advantages of both FeNi LDHs and MOFs for the OER, we develop an in situ strategy for fabricating 2D/1D FeNi LDH-MOF hybrid arrays as high-performance OER catalysts by partially transforming bulk FeNi LDH into the corresponding FeNi MOF. Interestingly, the formed ultrathin 2D nanosheets have a thickness of only about $1.34\mathrm{nm}$ , and the 1D sword-like MOF nanorods have a length of $1.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ beyond nanosheets. Further studies demonstrated that the formation of FeNi nanosheets/MOFs should result from the following two key processes: etching of FeNi-LDH into ultrathin 2D nanosheets and heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs into 1D sword-like crystals. The in situ obtained 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF arrays can provide abundant exposed active sites and large contact areas, and thus accelerated charge transfer and mass transformation rates. The combined advantages of the FeNi 2D/1D structures lead to an excellent OER catalytic activity, displaying a low overpotential of $272~\mathrm{mV}$ at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , a small Tafel slope of $34.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ , at least longterm durability of $36\mathrm{h}$ and $10000\mathrm{CV}$ cycles under $1.0\mathrm{~m~}$ KOH.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Preparation and Characterizations of the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/ MOF Arrays
For synthesizing the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF arrays, a two-step strategy is developed, as illustrated in Figure 1a. First, the FeNi layered double hydroxide nanosheets were uniformly rooted onto the surface of CC (FeNi-LDH) through a hydrothermal process with $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ (the mole ratio of Fe: Ni is
1: 3) as the metal sources. The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns of the CC substrate and 2D FeNi-LDH are displayed in Figure 1b. The broad peaks positioned at $8.9^{\circ}$ , $25.3^{\circ}$ , and $43.3^{\circ}$ belong to the carbon matrix. The peaks at $11.5^{\circ}$ , $23.3^{\circ}$ , $34.6^{\circ}$ , $39^{\circ}$ , $46.4^{\circ}$ , $59.7^{\circ}$ , $61.1^{\circ}$ , and $65.2^{\circ}$ can be assigned to (003), (006), (012), (015), (018), (110), (113), and (116) facets of FeNi-LDH (PDF#51-0463), respectively, confirming the successful synthesis of FeNi-LDH. The SEM image (Figure 1c) and TEM image (Figure 1d) indicate that the formed FeNi-LDHs have regular 2D nanosheets morphology with a very smooth surface. As shown in Figure 1e, the lattice spacings of 0.23 and $0.26\mathrm{nm}$ correspond to (015) and (012) plane of FeNi-LDH, well consistent with the XRD results. Additionally, the semitransparent quality under the electron beams (Figure  1d) and a thickness of about ${\approx}9.84~\mathrm{nm}$ from atomic force microscopy (AFM) results (Figure  1f,g) indicate the ultrathin feature of the FeNi-LDH nanosheets, which is beneficial for the diffusion of organic ligands through the surface of FeNi-LDH, ensuring the sufficient coordination reaction between metal ions and organic ligands. From the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra (Figure 2a,b), $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions are coexistent in the nanosheets for the whole charge balance. Noticeably, altering the concentration of metal salts aqueous solution and the mole ratio of Fe: Ni, the thickness of LDH nanosheets and the coverage density on carbon fiber can be easily tuned (Figures S1,S2, Supporting Information).
In the second step, 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid $\mathrm{^{\prime}H_{4}D O B D C})$ was added, which then coordinated with the asprepared FeNi-LDH to allow for in situ phase reconstruction and nanocrystal growth, giving rising to the formation of CCsupported 2D FeNi-LDH nanosheets grafted with 1D swordlike FeNi-MOF crystals (2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF). To examine the morphology evolution of FeNi-LDH during coordination reaction with $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , SEM images of the samples based on the time-dependent reaction are shown in Figure  3. For convenience, other samples are signed as ${\mathrm{ZMOF}}x{\cdot}Y$ , which $x$ is the concentration of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , $\gamma$ is the reaction time. Thus, the obtained samples with reaction time of 1, 2, and 3 days are denoted as ZMOF4-1, 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF, and MOF4-3, respectively. From the results shown in Figures 3d–f and 4a,b, after 1 day reaction, the MOF4-1 sample shows apparently different surface structure compared with the original FeNi-LDH (thinness $4.82\ \mathrm{nm}$ vs $9.48~\mathrm{nm}$ ), and the surface color changed from yellow to black (Figure S3a,b, Supporting Information). It is noteworthy that due to the small content of MOF-74 in the MOF4-1, no obvious diffraction signals of MOF-74 can be observed in the XRD pattern of MOF4-1 (Figure  4c). These results imply that there is only a small amount MOF-74 coating on the surface of FeNi-LDH. With prolonging the coordination reaction time (Figure  3g–I), 1D sword-like crystals began to form and the nanosheets become much thinner. From Figure  4d–f, we can find that the thickness of the nanosheets is only $1.34\mathrm{nm}$ , much thinner than the previously reported 2D materials.[11c,15,16]
Such ultrathin FeNi nanosheets can provide more exposed active surfaces for electrocatalysis. Figure S4a-c, Supporting Information demonstrates that the formed 1D MOF crystals are also very thin $(10-25~\mathrm{nm}$ , Figure S4c, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the 1D crystals are about $250~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter and around $1.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in length beyond the nanosheets (Figure 3i). These results suggest that the ultrathin nanosheets combined with the 1D sword-like crystals can provide more surface areas for electrocatalytic reaction and for mass transport by sufficiently contacting with the electrolyte. Meanwhile, the XRD pattern indicates that the formed 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF sample is the hybrid of FeNi-LDH and MOF-74 (Figure 4c). As coordination reaction time further increases (Figure  3j–l), the nanosheets are almost invisible and the 1D sword-like crystals densely covered on the surface of carbon cloth. The XRD pattern also shows that only the diffraction signals of MOF-74 can be observed (Figure  4c), demonstrating little residual of FeNi-LDH in the sample of ZMOF4-3.
Based on the above structural characterizations, we propose that there are two main processes involved in the formation of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF hybrid: FeNi-LDH etching (dissolution reaction) and heterogeneous nucleation and growth of the metal-organic framework material (nucleation reaction). With the dissolving of metal ions from the surface of FeNi-LDH, the nanosheets become thinner and thinner. On the other hand, the organic ligands simultaneously coordinate with metal ions on the surface of FeNi-LDH and in the solution, thus resulting in the formation of MOF-74 first on the surface of FeNi-LDH and then on the carbon cloth. With reaction time increasing, the FeNi-LDH nanosheets become thinner, and simultaneously thicker and longer MOF-74 crystals are formed. The morphology evolution of FeNi-LDH during coordination process mainly experience three stages, as graphically illustrated in Figure $3\mathrm{m-p}$ .
To further confirm the phase transformation from FeNiLDH to MOF-74, samples based on different concentrations of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ ligands were also prepared. For the formation of MOF-74, the nucleation rate should have a positive correlation with the concentration of organic ligands. As shown in Figures S5 and S6, Supporting Information, under lower concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ , since the nucleation rate is too slow, only the surface color of nanosheets changed to black and FeNi-LDH but not MOF-74 crystal phase can be seen in the XRD pattern (ZMOF2-2), just like ZMOF4-1 (Figure 4c). However, with higher $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ concentration, the coordination reaction rate is too high, thus leading to the transformation of almost all FeNi-LDH to 1D sword-like MOF-74 (ZMOF6-2), similar to ZMOF4-3 (Figure  4c). These control experiments clearly indicate that, similar to the reaction time-dependent results, the final crystal phase of the product is also strongly dependent on the concentration of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ ligands. With $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ concentration increasing, the phase transformation rate increases until all the NiFe LDH nanosheets are transformed to MOF-74.
In order to unveil the detail microstructure of the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF, TEM characterization was further performed. In accord with the SEM results, the low-magnified TEM image shows the presence of both 2D nanosheets and 1D sword-like crystals (Figure  5a). From the high-resolution TEM images and SAED pattern (Figure 5b-c and Figure S7a,b, Supporting Information), the observed lattice spacings of 0.15, 0.23, and $0.26\ \mathrm{nm}$ correspond to the (113), (015), and (012) planes of FeNi-LDH, respectively. Meanwhile, the d-spacings of 0.13, 0.17, 0.19, 0.21, 0.28, 0.31, and $0.33~\mathrm{nm}$ agree well with the XRD peaks of MOF-74 at $72.1^{\circ}$ , $53.8^{\circ}$ , $47.2^{\circ}$ , $42.5^{\circ}$ , $31.7^{\circ}$ , $29^{\circ}$ , and $27^{\circ}$ , respectively, in the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF sample. These crystal features further confirm that the 2D/1D NiFe LDH/MOF is a hybrid composed with FeNi-LDH and MOF74. Notably, there is an obvious crystal boundary between LDH and MOF (Figure 5b), implying their strong interaction. From the HAADF-STEM images, line scans and elemental mappings of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF (Figure 5d–I and Figure S8, Supporting Information), we can find that the elements of Ni, Fe are mainly distributed in the 2D LDH nanosheets and 1D sword-like MOF crystals, and C, O are uniformly in the whole sample. Interestingly, as shown in Figure S9, Supporting Information, the mole ratio of Ni/Fe in FeNi-LDH is around 2.93, while in 2D/1D LDH/MOF is about 2.50 and in ZMOF4-3 is about 2.44. These results indicate that $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is preferentially dissolved from the surface of FeNi-LDH to form MOF-74, in accord with our previously reported FeNiMOFs that $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is easier than $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to coordinate with carboxyl group.[7b,17]
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were finally carried out to have an insight into the elemental composition and chemical states in the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF. As shown in Figure 2a,b, the deconvoluted XPS of Fe 2p and Ni 2p confirm that the metal ions keep the chemical states of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , but their binding energies from the 2D/1D FeNi $\mathrm{\DeltaLDH/}$ MOF show an obvious downshift (0.3 and $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) compared with FeNi-LDH. The different binding energy shifts of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ could be attributed to the increased mole ratio of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF than FeNi-LDH (1:2.50  vs 1:2.93, Figure S9, Supporting Information). The Brunauer− Emmett−Teller (BET) measurements show that after phase transformation from FeNi-LDH to 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF, the surface area obviously increases from 20.9 to $35.0\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , as shown in Figure 4f. Besides, the mean pore size of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF is also larger than that of FeNi-LDH (Figure S5b, Supporting Information, $4.44\ \mathrm{nm}$ vs $3.06~\mathrm{nm}$ ). Therefore, it is anticipated that with the fabricated 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF as electrocatalyst for the OER, more catalytically active sites will be exposed, and the mass transfer rates of reactants and products will be enhanced.
## 2.2. Electrocatalytic Activities of the As-Prepared 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF toward the OER
Encouraged by the unique hierarchical structure, the intrinsic catalytic activity of the original 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF for oxygen evolution reaction (OER) was assessed in $1.0\textrm{M}\mathrm{KOH}$ without the post-calcination process. For comparison, the precursor of FeNi-LDH, 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF prepared with different coordination reaction times and $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ concentrations as well as commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ were also evaluated and a $95\%$ iR correction was applied to all measurements. As shown in Figure 6a and Figure S10a, Supporting Information, all the prepared MOF-incorporated electrocatalysts display lower overpotentials and higher current densities for the OER in comparison with the FeNi-LDH precursor and the commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , demonstrating that the MOF-74 introduction is highly favorable for the OER process. However, the OER catalytic activities of the prepared materials are dependent on the synthesis conditions. Along with prolonging the coordination reaction time with $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ , the OER catalytic activities of the products show a volcanic-type variation (Figure 6c). After reaction for 2 days, the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF sample displays the best electrocatalytic property with the lowest overpotential value of $272~\mathrm{mV}$ at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and the smallest Tafel slop of $34.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which are significantly less than those of the FeNi-LDH $329\ \mathrm{mV},$ $64.6\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ ), ZMOF4-1 ( $321~\mathrm{mV},$ $52.9~\mathrm{mV}$ dec–1), ZMOF4-3 $(300\ \mathrm{mV},\ 51.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ , and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(465\ \mathrm{mV},\ 77.5\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ 1 (Figure  6a–c). Furthermore, such overpotential and Tafel slop from the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF are lower than most of the previously reported MOF-based OER electrocatalysts (Table S1, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/ MOF shows the largest current density of $276{\mathrm{~mA~cm}}^{-2}$ at the overpotential of $300\mathrm{mV},$ compared to those obtained from other samples ( $50.1\mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ from FeNi-LDH, $46.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ from
MOF4-1, $100.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ from MOF4-3 and $5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ from $\mathrm{RuO}_{2})$ ). In another hand, similar to the effect of coordination reaction time, the OER activity of the composite also displays a volcanic-type variation with the increase of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ ligand concentration, and the sample synthesized with $4~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ (2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF) shows the best OER catalytic performance. Meanwhile, the effect of $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ molar ratio on the OER catalytic activity of the 2D/1D hybrid nanostructure was also examined. As shown in Figure S11, Supporting Information, the sample with the Fe/Ni molar ratio of 1/3 shows the highest OER catalytic activity. Therefore, the 2D/1D hybrid nanostructure was synthesized with the optimal Fe/Ni molar ratio of $1/3$ .
To get a deeper insight into the partial underlying reason for the exceptional OER catalytic activity of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/ MOF, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and the electrochemical double-layer capacitances $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ were further collected and studied. Obviously, the FeNi 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF shows outstanding electronic conductivity with the lowest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value of $4.12\ \Omega$ (Figure  6d and Figure S12, Supporting Information), confirming the enhanced electron transfer ability with the incorporation of MOF crystals. It should be noted that the original FeNi-LDH has a lower $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value than ZMOF4-1 and ZMOF2-2, but its OER activity is still lower than the two samples. These results suggest that the catalytic activity of the ZMOF sample is dependent on not only the formation of MOF crystals but also the structure of FeNi-LDH nanosheets. Compared to the original FeNi-LDH, the much thinner nanosheets and the introduction of porous MOF in ZMOF4-1 and ZMOF2-2 can not only accelerate the mass transport of reactants and products, but also provide more exposed catalytic active sites. In addition, the double layer capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ evaluation from cyclic voltammogram (CV) curves versus different scan rates (Figure  6e, Figures S10d and S13, Supporting Information) indicates that the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF has much larger $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ $(1.82~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}})$ ) than other samples. Such result suggests that the FeNi 2D/1D LDH/MOF has the largest electrochemical active surface area and the richest potential active sites, which is favorable to show desirable intrinsic activity for the OER.
For nanostructured catalysts, long-term durability is also an important parameter to evaluate their real application potential. Here, the stability of the prepared 2D/1D FeNi $\mathrm{\DeltaLDH/}$ MOF was assessed by chronopotentiometric curves and CV cycling tests without iR compensation. As shown in Figure 6f, the current density of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF shows a negligible loss (only $7\%$ loss) after $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ of durability test, demonstrating the excellent catalytic stability of the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF catalyst in strong basic media. Moreover, as shown in Figure 6f inset, the LSV curve only gives a negligible positive shift ( $20~\mathrm{mV}$ at $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}},$ ) after 10 000 potential cycles, further confirming the high durability. Furthermore, the morphology and metal chemical states of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF after $10~000~\mathrm{CV}$ cycles were also examined by TEM and XPS. As shown in Figure S14a, Supporting Information, the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF still maintains the original morphology with 2D ultrathin nanosheets and 1D sword-like crystals. The HRTEM and SAED pattern also indicate the presence of both FeN-LDH and MOF-74 in the composite of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF (Figure S14b-e Supporting Information). The XPS results (Figure S14f-h, Supporting Information) show that except for the partial oxidation of Fe and Ni induced by the OER process, the two metals mainly exist with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ chemical states. From the XRD patterns shown in Figure S15, Supporting Information, it can be seen that after ADT test, both FeN-LDH and MOF-74 are still present in the 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF composite, agreeing well with the TEM and XPS results. However, compared to the FeN-LDH, the diffraction intensities from MOFs are significantly reduced. These results indicate that part of MOFs phase may be reconverted to LDH phase during the OER in alkaline electrolyte, which is consistent with the previous reports.[7b,19] The above characterizations clearly indicate that the in situ formed 2D/1D hybrid nanostructure has highly structure and electrochemical stability. The enhanced electrocatalytic activity and durability make this type of catalyst have promising application in electrochemical water splitting.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a facile strategy is developed for in situ transformation of 2D FeNi-LDH nanosheets to 2D/1D FeNi $\mathrm{\DeltaLDH/}$ MOF hybrid nanostructure for efficient OER. The structure and catalytic properties of the 2D/1D composite can be effectively tuned by the coordination reaction time and the added ligand concentration. Under reaction time of 2 days and $4~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ concentration of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , ultrathin and uniform 2D nanosheets ( $1.34\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness) and long 1D sword-like MOF crystals $(1.3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length) were formed in the LDH-MOF hybrid. The produced 2D FeNi LDH nanosheets combined with 1D porous FeNi MOF crystals can provide abundant exposed active sites and enhanced mass transport channels for the electrocatalysis. As a result, the prepared 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF exhibited excellent OER catalytic activity compared with FeNiLDH precursor and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . The optimal 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF showed the lowest overpotential of $272~\mathrm{mV}$ at $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and the smallest Tafel slop of $34.1~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ . Meanwhile, the optimized composite has high electrocatalytic stability with the OER activity remains almost unchanged over $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $10\ 000\ \mathrm{CV}$ cycles. This work not only provides a facile strategy for preparation of low-cost and efficient OER electrocatalysts, but also opens a new avenue for preparation of ultrathin LDH nanosheets combined with FeNi MOF crystals.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis of FeNi-LDH Arrays: Before using, the purchased carbon cloth (CC) was washed by ethanol and deionized water for at least three times in sequence under ultrasonic treatment to achieve a clear surface. Then the washed CC was further treated in nitric acid at ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2\mathfrak{h}$ to generate numbers of the carboxyl and oxhydryl groups on the CC surface. The resulting CC substrate was completely cleaned with water and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight. The FeNi-LDH/CC arrays were then prepared following a previously reported procedure with a slight modification, as shown in detail in the Supporting Information.[20]
Synthesis of 2D/1D FeNi LDH/MOF Arrays: Typically, DI water $(7m L)$ was added into the mixture of 2, 5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{C})$ and DMF $(4\mathrm{~mg\mL^{-1}})$ to obtain a homogeneous solution in a Teflon-lined autoclave $(25~\mathsf{m L})$ . Subsequently, a piece of FeNi-LDH $(1\times1\mathsf{c m})$ template was immersed into the above solution. The autoclave was maintained at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 2 days. The sample was then collected and washed with absolute ethanol several times and then dried in an oven at $50~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to obtain the target product. To perform the hydrothermal timedependent experiments, a series of reaction times including 1 and 3 days were employed. In addition, the ligand concentration-dependent experiments were also performed with 2 and $6~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ of ${\mathsf{H}}_{4}{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{C}}/$ DMF solutions. For convenience, the products were donated as $Z M O F x-y,$ $x$ is the used concentration of $H_{4}D O B D C$ and $\gamma$ refers to the reaction time.
Materials Characterizations: The morphologies and compositions of the as-prepared catalysts were characterized on an XL30 ESEMFEG scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) and the techniques of energy dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) with accelerating voltage of $20{\mathrm{~kV}}.$ High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images were measured by a JEM-2010 (HR) microscope operated at $200\ \mathsf{k V}.$ For SEM and HRTEM measurements, the samples were prepared by dropping ethanol dispersion of samples onto silicon wafer or carboncoated copper TEM grids using pipettes and dried under ambient condition. The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were performed on a D8 ADVANCE (BRUKER Germany) with $\mathsf{C u-K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=7.54\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ ). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were conducted on a VG Thermo ESCALAB 250 spectrometer (VG Scientific) operated at 120 W. The Barrett-Emmett-Teller (BET) measurements were carried out on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 System using automatic volumetric adsorption equipment.
Electrochemical Measurements: All the electrochemical experiments were performed in a standard three-electrode electrochemical cell by a CHI 750D electrochemical workstation under ambient atmosphere using ${\mathsf{\textsf{l}}}{\mathsf{\textsf{M}}}{\mathsf{K}}{\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{H}}$ aqueous solution as electrolyte. The counter electrode and reference electrode are a graphite plate and an $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ with saturated KCl solution, respectively. All the data of linear sweep voltammetry were corrected with $95\%\ i R_{s}$ drop unless otherwise noted, where $R_{s}$ is the contact resistance between electrode and solution. The accelerated durability tests (ADTs) were performed by chronopotentiometric curves at the potential corresponding to the current density of around $10\ m A\ c m^{-2}$ and by cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests between 1.1 and $\mathsf{1.5~V}$ (vs RHE) under $10\ 000$ cycles at a scan rate of $\mathsf{l}00\ m\vee\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in ${\mathsf{1}}{\mathsf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{M}}$ KOH. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) experiments were performed at $\overline{{1.5~\vee}}$ with a frequency range between $70~\mathsf{K H z}$ and $0.01~\mathsf{H z}$ and an AC amplitude of $5~\mathsf{m V}.$ The electrochemical double-layer capacitances were measurement between 1.1 and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.3\vee}}$ (Vs. RHE) at scan rates from 5 to $200\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ .
